[[File:Kamado-jinja torii-1.JPG|thumb|200px|right|First Torii of the Lower Shrine]]
'''Kamado Shrine''' (竈門神社, Kamado-jinja) is a {{ill|lt=Shinto shrine|Q845945|qid=Q845945|1=Shinto shrine}} located in {{ill|lt=Dazaifu City|Q728218|qid=Q728218|1=Dazaifu, Fukuoka}}, {{ill|lt=Fukuoka Prefecture|Q123258|qid=Q123258|1=Fukuoka Prefecture}}. It is a {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=Shikinaisha|1=Shikinaisha|qid=Q134917286}} ({{ill|lt=Myōjin Taisha|Q9610964|qid=Q9610964|1=Myōjin Taisha}}). Under the {{ill|lt=modern shrine ranking system|Q712534|qid=Q712534|1=Modern system of ranked Shinto shrines}}, it was formerly ranked as a {{ill|lt=Modern shrine ranking system#Government shrines|Q712534|qid=Q712534|lt=Kanpei-shōsha|1=Modern system of ranked Shinto shrines}}, and is currently a {{ill|lt=Beppyo Shrine|Q10898274|qid=Q10898274|1=Beppyo shrine}} of the {{ill|lt=Association of Shinto Shrines|Q908077|qid=Q908077|1=Association of Shinto Shrines}}. It is also known as “'''Hōmangū'''” and “Kamado-gū.”<ref name="地名">“Kamado Shrine” entry, *Place Names of Fukuoka Prefecture*.</ref>

=== Overview ===
The shrine is located on {{ill|lt=Mount Hōman|Q11452604|qid=Q11452604|1=Mount Hōman}}, which rises to the northeast of {{ill|lt=Dazaifu City|Q728218|qid=Q728218|1=Dazaifu, Fukuoka}}, and its buildings are divided into two main sites:
* '''[[#Mount Hōman (Upper Shrine and Middle Shrine Ruins)|Upper Shrine]]''' – at the summit of Mount Hōman (elevation 829.6 m).
* '''[[#Mountain Foot (Lower Shrine)|Lower Shrine]]''' – at the foot of Mount Hōman.

In the past, there was also a Middle Shrine on the mountainside, but it was abolished during the Meiji period.

Because Mount Hōman lies to the northeast—the {{ill|lt=kimon|Q11673539|qid=Q11673539|1=Kimon}} (demon gate)—of {{ill|lt=Dazaifu|Q144845|qid=Q144845|1=Dazaifu (government)}}, the shrine was revered as a guardian deity protecting Dazaifu. From the {{ill|lt=Heian period|Q193292|qid=Q193292|1=Heian period}} onward, {{ill|lt=Shinbutsu-shūgō|Q2279088|qid=Q2279088|1=Shinbutsu-shūgō}} (the syncretism of Shinto and Buddhism) progressed, and the shrine became integrated with its {{ill|lt=jingū-ji|Q1074971|qid=Q1074971|1=Jingū-ji}} temple, Daisen-ji (also known as Kamado-san-ji or Uchi-yama-ji), which was a representative {{ill|lt=Tendai|Q668188|qid=Q668188|1=Tiantai}} temple in western Japan.<ref name="角川"/> In addition, Mount Hōman, together with {{ill|lt=Mount Hiko|Q3077886|qid=Q3077886|1=Mount Hiko}}, developed into one of the major training grounds of {{ill|lt=Shugendō|Q1483875|qid=Q1483875|1=Shugendō}}. These Buddhist facilities were abolished in the Meiji period; however, even today there are about 40 shrines in Fukuoka Prefecture that were {{ill|lt=kanjō|Q123923583|qid=Q123923583|1=Seichi junrei}}-enshrined from this shrine,<ref>“Mount Hōman” entry, *Kadokawa Dictionary of Japanese Place Names: Fukuoka Prefecture*.</ref> and it continues to be worshipped for faith in Mount Hōman and for matchmaking blessings.

As cultural properties, the entire Mount Hōman area including the shrine grounds is designated a national {{ill|lt=Historic Site|Q1081138|qid=Q1081138|1=Historic site}}. In addition, wooden komainu (guardian lion-dogs) and 307 items related to mountain worship on Mount Hōman are designated cultural properties of Fukuoka Prefecture.

=== About the Shrine Name ===
[[File:Homan zan 01.JPG|thumb|200px|right|{{ill|lt=Mount Hōman|Q11452604|qid=Q11452604|1=Mount Hōman}}<br /><small>Because of its shape, it was formerly also called “Mount Mikasa.”</small>]]
The shrine is enshrined on {{ill|lt=Mount Hōman|Q11452604|qid=Q11452604|1=Mount Hōman}} (829.6 m), northeast of {{ill|lt=Dazaifu|Q144845|qid=Q144845|1=Dazaifu (government)}}. In ancient times, this mountain was known as “Mount Mikasa” and “Mount Kamado.”<ref name="宝満山">“Mount Hōman” entry, *Place Names of Fukuoka Prefecture*.</ref> The name “Mount Mikasa” is said to derive from its hat-like shape,<ref name="神々"/> and it is also believed to have been worshipped as a {{ill|lt=shintai mountain|Q1595289|qid=Q1595289|1=Sacred mountains}} in ancient times.<ref name="角川"/>

The other name, “Mount Kamado,” appears frequently in poetry as a {{ill|lt=utamakura|Q1588579|qid=Q1588579|1=Utamakura}} of {{ill|lt=Chikuzen Province|Q1047664|qid=Q1047664|1=Chikuzen Province}}.<ref name="地名"/> According to the shrine tradition recorded in the late {{ill|lt=Kamakura period|Q236205|qid=Q236205|1=Kamakura period}} text *Kamado Hōman Daibosatsu-ki*, the mountain was originally called “Buttō-san” or “Mount Mikasa,” but gained the name “Kamado” because {{ill|lt=Empress Jingū|Q232803|qid=Q232803|1=Empress Jingū}} is said to have set up a hearth (kamado) on the mountain at the time of childbirth.<ref name="宝満山"/> On the other hand, the *{{ill|lt=Chikuzen no Kuni Zoku Fudoki|Q108571667|qid=Q108571667|1=Chikuzen no Kuni Zoku Fudoki}}* explains the name as deriving from the mountain’s resemblance to a {{ill|lt=kamado (hearth)|Q12177411|qid=Q12177411|1=Kamado}}, and from the constant clouds and mist that look like rising cooking smoke.<ref name="宝満山"/> Other theories include that a {{ill|lt=Kamado deity|Q249178|qid=Q249178|1=Kitchen God}} was enshrined for the protection of Dazaifu,<ref name="神々"/> or that the name derives from “Kamado Rock” near the ninth station of the mountain.<ref name="神々"/>

The current name “Mount Hōman” is said to originate from the syncretic designation of the enshrined deity as “Hōman Daibosatsu,”<ref name="由緒書"/> with the earliest documentary appearance dating to around the late 13th century.<ref name="由緒書"/>

=== Enshrined Deities ===
'''Primary deity'''
* '''{{ill|lt=Tamayorihime (Hyūga mythology)|Q3082751|qid=Q3082751|lt=Tamayorihime-no-Mikoto|1=Tamayori-hime (mother of Jimmu)}}'''<ref>The list of deities follows the shrine’s official history.</ref>

'''Associated deities'''
* {{ill|lt=Empress Jingū|Q232803|qid=Q232803|1=Empress Jingū}}
* {{ill|lt=Emperor Ōjin|Q317997|qid=Q317997|1=Emperor Ōjin}} – the 15th emperor, son of Empress Jingū.

Among these, Tamayorihime-no-Mikoto is considered the original enshrined deity, while Empress Jingū and Emperor Ōjin were later added.<ref name="地名"/> Sources such as the *Hakozaki Shrine Engi* regard this shrine as the aunt of the {{ill|lt=Hachiman deity|Q261637|qid=Q261637|1=Hachiman}} (aunt of Emperor Ōjin and elder sister of Empress Jingū), suggesting that by at least the early {{ill|lt=12th century|Q7061|qid=Q7061|1=12th century}}, the shrine had become associated with Hachiman worship.<ref name="地名"/> Scholars point out that this incorporation into the Hachiman lineage reflects the shrine’s long-standing political ties with Dazaifu.<ref name="神々"/>

On the other hand, some theories hold that the original deity implied by the shrine’s name was the hearth god {{ill|lt=Sanbō Kōjin|Q13577821|qid=Q13577821|1=Sanbo Kojin}}.<ref>{{ill|lt=Hōga Toshio|Q11452691|qid=Q11452691|1=Toshio Hōga}}, “A Tentative Theory on the Location of Fumi Province,” *Kojiki no Heya*, 2002.</ref>

In medieval times the deity was also called “Hōman Daibosatsu,” and in the early modern period “Hōman Myōjin.”<ref name="国史">*Kokushi Daijiten*, entry on Kamado Shrine.</ref>

=== History ===
=== Founding ===
[[File:Dazaifu (government) seiden.JPG|thumb|200px|right|[[Dazaifu|Remains of the Dazaifu Government Hall]]<br /><small>The shrine lies to its {{ill|lt=kimon|Q11673539|qid=Q11673539|1=Kimon}} (northeast).</small>]]
According to shrine tradition, when {{ill|lt=Dazaifu|Q144845|qid=Q144845|1=Dazaifu (government)}} was relocated to its current site during the reign of {{ill|lt=Emperor Tenji|Q314802|qid=Q314802|1=Emperor Tenji}} ({{ill|lt=668|Q30116|qid=Q30116|1=668}}–{{ill|lt=672|Q30601|qid=Q30601|1=672}}), the {{ill|UNKNOWN|lt=Yaoyorozu no kami|1=Yaoyorozu no Kami|qid=Q118906758}} were enshrined on {{ill|lt=Mount Hōman|Q11452604|qid=Q11452604|1=Mount Hōman}}, located to the northeast (kimon), to protect Dazaifu, marking the beginning of the shrine’s worship.<ref name="由緒書"/><ref name="神々">*Gods of Japan*, entry on Kamado Shrine.</ref> Later, in the 2nd year of {{ill|lt=Emperor Tenmu|Q335181|qid=Q335181|1=Emperor Tenmu}} ({{ill|lt=673|Q31105|qid=Q31105|1=673}}), the monk Shinren, while training in the mountains, encountered Tamayorihime-no-Mikoto. He reported this to the imperial court, and the Upper Shrine was built on the summit.<ref name="由緒書"/><ref name="神々"/> The shrine regards this event as its official founding.<ref name="由緒書"/>

Although the historical accuracy of these traditions is unclear,<ref name="神々"/> investigations of foundation stones at the Lower Shrine suggest that the shrine dates back at least to the latter half of the {{ill|lt=8th century|Q8086|qid=Q8086|1=8th century}}.<ref name="地名"/> In addition, many ritual artifacts—including {{ill|lt=Haji ware|Q5639938|qid=Q5639938|1=Haji pottery}} and {{ill|lt=imperial coins|Q11581072|qid=Q11581072|1=Kocho Junisen}}—dating from the {{ill|lt=9th century|Q8083|qid=Q8083|1=9th century}} through the medieval period have been found near the Upper Shrine, indicating connections with {{ill|lt=Dazaifu|Q144845|qid=Q144845|1=Dazaifu (government)}} and the {{ill|lt=Japanese missions to Tang China|Q262529|qid=Q262529|1=Japanese missions to Tang China}}.<ref name="由緒書"/><ref name="神々"/>

As reflected in shrine tradition, the history of Kamado Shrine developed in close relation to Dazaifu.<ref name="神々"/> Another theory, however, suggests that Tamayorihime-no-Mikoto was originally worshipped naturally as a {{ill|lt=water deity|Q11129547|qid=Q11129547|1=Mikumari}} at the sources of the Mikasa and Hōman rivers, and only later acquired political significance.<ref name="神々"/>

As can be seen in the shrine traditions, the history of this shrine developed while maintaining a deep relationship with Dazaifu <ref name="神々"/>. On the other hand, based on the character of Tamayorihime-no-Mikoto as a “{{ill|lt=water deity|Q11129547|qid=Q11129547|lt=deity of water|1=Mikumari}},” there is also a theory that Tamayorihime-no-Mikoto came to be enshrined naturally as the deity of the water sources of the Mikasa River and Hōman River, and that a political divine character was later bestowed upon her <ref name="神々"/>.

=== General History ===
==== From the Nara period to the Heian period ====
The earliest reliable historical record related to this shrine is an entry concerning Kamadosan-ji Temple (竈門山寺; the shrine’s {{ill|lt=jingū-ji|Q1074971|qid=Q1074971|1=Jingū-ji}}) from Enryaku 22 ({{ill|lt=803|Q30388|qid=Q30388|1=803}}) <ref group="原" name="延暦22">“Eizan Daishi Den,” entry for the intercalary 10th month, 23rd day of Enryaku 22 (803). Also appears in “Fusō Ryakki,” “Kongyoku Shōchūshō,” etc.</ref> <ref name="地名"/>. As this record shows, the shrine is thought to have already been in a state of {{ill|lt=shinbutsu-shūgō|Q2279088|qid=Q2279088|1=Shinbutsu-shūgō}} (syncretism of Shinto and Buddhism) by the {{ill|lt=Nara period|Q189178|qid=Q189178|1=Nara period}} <ref name="神々"/>.

Regarding “Kamado-no-Kami,” the earliest mention is an article from Shōwa 7 ({{ill|lt=840|Q29936|qid=Q29936|1=840}}) <ref group="原" name="承和7"/> noting the bestowal of the divine rank of Junior Fifth Rank, Upper Grade. Thereafter, the divine rank rose to Senior Fifth Rank, Upper Grade (or possibly Junior Fifth Rank, Upper Grade?) in Kajō 3 ({{ill|lt=850|Q30364|qid=Q30364|1=850}}) <ref group="原" name="嘉祥3"/>; Junior Fourth Rank, Lower Grade in Jōgan 1 ({{ill|lt=859|Q23897|qid=Q23897|1=859}}) <ref group="原" name="貞観元"/>; Junior Fourth Rank, Upper Grade in Gangyō 3 ({{ill|lt=879|Q25001|qid=Q25001|1=879}}) <ref group="原" name="元慶3"/>; and Senior Fourth Rank, Upper Grade in Kanpyō 8 ({{ill|lt=896|Q30465|qid=Q30465|1=896}}) <ref group="原" name="寛平8"/> <ref name="地名"/>.

In addition, there is a record of offerings in Shōwa 9 ({{ill|lt=842|Q24730|qid=Q24730|1=842}}) <ref group="原" name="承和9">“Shoku Nihon Kōki,” entry for the 3rd day of the 7th month of Shōwa 9 (842) (see Kokugakuin University “Shintō and Shrine Historical Sources Collection”).</ref> <ref name="地名"/>. In the “{{ill|lt=Engishiki|Q1342448|qid=Q1342448|1=Engishiki}}” {{ill|lt=Register of Deities|Q11064932|qid=Q11064932|1=Engishiki Jinmyōchō}} compiled in Engi 5 ({{ill|lt=927|Q30470|qid=Q30470|1=927}}), it is listed under Mikasa District of {{ill|lt=Chikuzen Province|Q1047664|qid=Q1047664|1=Chikuzen Province}} as “Kamado Shrine, Myōjin Taisha,” ranking it among the {{ill|lt=Myōjin Taisha|Q9610964|qid=Q9610964|1=Myōjin Taisha}} <ref name="地名"/>. The reading of the shrine name at the time is generally given as “Kamato,” though some manuscripts read it as “Kamakato” <ref name="地名"/>.

Regarding the shrine buildings, records begin with a fire in Chōji 2 ({{ill|lt=1105|Q13719|qid=Q13719|1=1105}}), followed by accounts of divination and relocation, and then another fire and related petitions in Kyūju 2 ({{ill|lt=1155|Q19650|qid=Q19650|1=1155}}) <ref name="地名"/>. As for shrine administration, by Ten’en 2 ({{ill|lt=979|Q23784|qid=Q23784|1=979}}) the Ōmiya-shi also served as the head priest of the entire mountain, but by the late 11th century Shinto and Buddhism had become unified, and the bettō (administrative head) of Ōyama-dera Temple managed the entire mountain including this shrine. In Chōji 2 (1105), {{ill|lt=Enryaku-ji|Q917195|qid=Q917195|1=Enryaku-ji}} and {{ill|lt=Iwashimizu Hachimangū|Q710098|qid=Q710098|1=Iwashimizu Hachimangū}} disputed over the bettōship of Ōyama-dera, the shrine’s jingū-ji <ref name="地名"/>.

In Kajō 1 ({{ill|lt=1106|Q19508|qid=Q19508|1=1106}}) <ref group="原" name="嘉承元"/>, the shrine reached the highest divine rank of Senior First Rank, and it is said that Mount Hōman was at its peak during this period <ref name="神々"/>. There are also traditions describing the shrine at that time as the “General Guardian Shrine of the Nine Provinces” <ref name="国史"/>. Furthermore, documents related to {{ill|lt=Hachiman|Q261637|qid=Q261637|1=Hachiman}} state that Kamado-no-Kami was the aunt of Hachiman (Emperor Ōjin), pointing to the political influence of the shrine in connection with Dazaifu and its relationship with Hachiman <ref name="神々"/>.

==== From the Kamakura period onward ====
(For developments after the {{ill|lt=Kamakura period|Q236205|qid=Q236205|1=Kamakura period}}, see also the section [[#Jingū-ji|Jingū-ji Section]].)

After the Heian period, the shrine continued to be operated as a unified Shinto-Buddhist institution <ref name="角川"/>. As this progressed, from the {{ill|lt=Muromachi period|Q334845|qid=Q334845|1=Muromachi period}} onward the name “Hōman-gū” also came into use <ref name="地名"/>. Subsequently, the shrine was caught up in the warfare of the {{ill|lt=Sengoku period|Q204023|qid=Q204023|1=Sengoku period}} and its power declined significantly <ref name="地名"/>.

Entering the {{ill|lt=early modern period|Q5308718|qid=Q5308718|1=Early modern period}}, the {{ill|lt=Kobayakawa clan|Q2975298|qid=Q2975298|1=Kobayakawa clan}}, who ruled Chikuzen Province, revived it as a {{ill|lt=Shugendō|Q1483875|qid=Q1483875|1=Shugendō}} training ground from Tenshō 15 ({{ill|lt=1587|Q6744|qid=Q6744|1=1587}}). In Keichō 2 ({{ill|lt=1597|Q6790|qid=Q6790|1=1597}}), the main hall, worship hall, lecture hall, ascetic hall, and subsidiary shrines were rebuilt <ref name="地名"/>.

Subsequently, it also received deep reverence and donations from {{ill|lt=Kuroda Nagamasa|Q950496|qid=Q950496|1=Kuroda Nagamasa}}, who entered the province in Keichō 5 ({{ill|lt=1600|Q6720|qid=Q6720|1=1600}}), but in Kan’ei 18 ({{ill|lt=1641|Q6907|qid=Q6907|1=1641}}) most of the buildings were destroyed by fire <ref name="地名"/>. In Keian 3 ({{ill|lt=1650|Q6951|qid=Q6951|1=1650}}), the shrine buildings were rebuilt by {{ill|lt=Kuroda Tadayuki|Q11678600|qid=Q11678600|1=Tadayuki Kuroda}}, the second lord of the {{ill|lt=Fukuoka Domain|Q208766|qid=Q208766|1=Fukuoka Domain}} <ref name="地名"/>. Throughout the {{ill|lt=Edo period|Q184963|qid=Q184963|1=Edo period}}, the shrine lands amounted to 50 koku <ref name="地名"/>.

With the advent of the {{ill|lt=Meiji period|Q3125096|qid=Q3125096|1=Meiji era}}, Buddhist elements were eliminated through the {{ill|lt=separation of Shinto and Buddhism|Q1970535|qid=Q1970535|1=Shinbutsu bunri}} <ref name="角川">“Kadokawa Dictionary of Japanese Place Names: Fukuoka Prefecture,” entry for Kamado Shrine.</ref>. Under the modern shrine ranking system in Meiji 5 ({{ill|lt=1872|Q7753|qid=Q7753|1=1872}}), it was designated a {{ill|lt=village shrine|Q134917284|qid=Q134917284|1=Son-sha}}, and in Meiji 28 ({{ill|lt=1895|Q7844|qid=Q7844|1=1895}}) it was promoted to a {{ill|lt=government shrine, minor|Q712534|qid=Q712534|1=Modern system of ranked Shinto shrines}} <ref name="由緒書"/><ref name="神々"/>. After the war, it has been listed as a {{ill|lt=special shrine|Q10898274|qid=Q10898274|1=Beppyo shrine}} of the {{ill|lt=Association of Shinto Shrines|Q908077|qid=Q908077|1=Association of Shinto Shrines}}.

