=== Jingū-ji ===
This shrine once had a jingū-ji called Ōyama-dera <ref name="角川"/>. Its sect was {{ill|lt=Tendai|Q668188|qid=Q668188|1=Tiantai}}.

Ōyama-dera was said to have been a representative Tendai temple of western Japan <ref name="地名"/>. Its name is also recorded as Kamadosan-ji, Uchiyama-ji, or Uchizan-ji <ref name="角川"/>; however, it is not certain that these all refer to the same temple <ref name="地名"/>.

The earliest record concerning the jingū-ji is from Enryaku 22 ({{ill|lt=803|Q30388|qid=Q30388|1=803}}) <ref group="原" name="延暦22"/> <ref name="角川"/>. From the foundation stones at the Lower Shrine, it is thought to have been of considerable scale by the latter half of the 8th century <ref name="角川"/>. According to records, in Enryaku 22 (803), {{ill|lt=Saichō|Q449943|qid=Q449943|1=Saichō}} carved four statues of the Medicine Buddha at Kamadosan-ji for the four ships bound for Tang China <ref name="神々"/>. In Shōwa 14 ({{ill|lt=847|Q30354|qid=Q30354|1=847}}), {{ill|lt=Ennin|Q732282|qid=Q732282|1=Ennin}} recited sutras before the deity upon returning from China, and in Ninju 2 ({{ill|lt=852|Q30383|qid=Q30383|1=852}}), {{ill|lt=Enchin|Q1972505|qid=Q1972505|1=Enchin}} did the same <ref name="地名"/>. In Shōhei 3 ({{ill|lt=933|Q24014|qid=Q24014|1=933}}), it is said that the monk Shamyō Shōkaku built one of Enryaku-ji’s “Six Treasure Pagodas” <ref name="地名"/>.

By the mid-Heian period, and no later than Bunji 4 ({{ill|lt=1188|Q19732|qid=Q19732|1=1188}}), the temple had become integrated with Kamado Shrine and functioned as a shrine-temple <ref name="地名"/><ref name="角川"/>. Thereafter, by the end of the 11th century, Ōyama-dera temporarily became a branch temple of {{ill|lt=Iwashimizu Hachimangū|Q710098|qid=Q710098|1=Iwashimizu Hachimangū}}, but in the early 12th century it became a branch of {{ill|lt=Enryaku-ji|Q917195|qid=Q917195|1=Enryaku-ji}} on {{ill|lt=Mount Hiei|Q748040|qid=Q748040|1=Mount Hiei}} <ref name="角川"/>. As a result, the temple’s petitions reached even the central authorities in Kyoto <ref name="地名"/>. Around Kajō 1 (1106), when Kamado Shrine was granted Senior First Rank, the temple possessed numerous monks’ quarters and reached its peak together with Kamado-no-Kami <ref name="神々"/>. In the late Heian song collection “{{ill|lt=Ryōjin Hishō|Q3010584|qid=Q3010584|1=Ryōjin Hishō}},” it is praised as “the main mountain of Kamado,” one of the sacred sites of Chikuzen <ref name="地名"/>.

From the late Kamakura period into the Muromachi period, Mount Hōman became a center of Shugendō practice, forming peak-to-peak ascetic training with Mount Hiko as the Taizōkai and Mount Hōman as the Kongōkai <ref name="地名"/><ref name="神々"/>. Entering the {{ill|lt=Nanboku-chō period|Q264012|qid=Q264012|1=Nanboku-chō period}}, warfare associated with the construction of nearby castles such as Uchizan Castle and Hōman Castle led to a decline in the temple’s power <ref name="角川"/>. In Kōji 3 ({{ill|lt=1557|Q6595|qid=Q6595|1=1557}}), land surveys and destruction of halls by {{ill|lt=Ōtomo Sōrin|Q1185032|qid=Q1185032|1=Ōtomo Sōrin}} caused further decline; it is said that although there were once 370 monks’ quarters at its height, this had fallen to 25 by the early modern period <ref name="地名"/>.

Later, in the early modern period, the Kobayakawa and Kuroda clans revived it as a Shugendō training ground for yamabushi <ref name="地名"/>. However, due in part to the fire of Kan’ei 18 (1641), most temple buildings were lost, and by the mid-Edo period most surrounding monks’ quarters had disappeared <ref name="地名"/>. The remaining Buddhist facilities were completely eliminated during the Meiji anti-Buddhist movement <ref name="角川"/>.

=== Anime Pilgrimage Site ===
* {{ill|lt=Demon Slayer: Kimetsu no Yaiba|Q24862683|qid=Q24862683|1=Demon Slayer: Kimetsu no Yaiba}}: Although the precise relationship has not been clarified by Koyoharu Gotouge or Shueisha, the shrine has attracted attention as a possible pilgrimage site for “Demon Slayer” due to several common points: it shares the surname “Kamado” with the protagonist Kamado Tanjiro; the shrine is associated with sealing the {{ill|lt=demon gate|Q11673539|qid=Q11673539|1=Kimon}} of {{ill|lt=Dazaifu|Q144845|qid=Q144845|1=Dazaifu (government)}}; ascetics training on Mount Hōman wear garments with a {{ill|lt=checkered pattern|Q14290457|qid=Q14290457|1=Check (pattern)}} matching Tanjiro’s {{ill|lt=haori|Q1503993|qid=Q1503993|1=Haori}}; there is a grave of a swordsmith named Kongōbei; and the author {{ill|lt=Koyoharu Gotouge|Q24865213|qid=Q24865213|1=Koyoharu Gotouge}} is from Fukuoka Prefecture. From around November {{ill|lt=2019|Q25274|qid=Q25274|1=2019}}, visitor numbers increased sharply along with the popularity of “Demon Slayer” <ref name=":0">“Is it a sacred site of the popular manga ‘Demon Slayer’?” Nishinippon Shimbun (Jan. 29, 2020).</ref><ref name=":1">“‘Demon Slayer’ sacred sites? Visitors increase at ‘Kamado Shrines’ nationwide,” Nikkan Sports (Oct. 20, 2020).</ref>.
